i","years":[1996]}],"author_id":"A5064340656","ca_plastic_works_count":3,"ca_works_count":5,"citations_by_year":[{"all":4054,"plastic":0,"year":2017},{"all":3863,"plastic":0,"year":2018},{"all":3981,"plastic":0,"year":2019},{"all":3844,"plastic":0,"year":2020},{"all":4248,"plastic":0,"year":2021},{"all":3845,"plastic":0,"year":2022},{"all":3725,"plastic":6,"year":2023},{"all":3569,"plastic":10,"year":2024},{"all":3668,"plastic":22,"year":2025},{"all":3019,"plastic":22,"year":2026}],"cited_by_count":89721,"current_in_ca":true,"domain_tags":[],"early_career":false,"first_pub_year":1987,"first_year":2009,"h_index":155,"institution_ids":["I134085113","I142207940","I154570441","I2803209242"],"last_ca_year":2026,"last_year":2025,"m_quotient":3.97,"name":"Craig J. Hawker","orcid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9951-851X","plastic_basis":"openalex","plastic_citations":60,"plastic_works_count":3,"primary_affiliations":[{"country_code":"US","id":"I154570441","name":"University of California, Santa Barbara","source":"openalex_last_known"},{"country_code":"US","id":"I2803209242","name":"University of California System","source":"openalex_last_known"}],"rising_pct":69,"rising_score":0.0052,"work_count":5,"work_ids":["W2024495322","W2321215647","W4306811487","W4398245957","W4412132509"]},"schema_version":"s3.1","works":[{"abstract":"We report the performance and characterization of a material based on poly[(3-mercaptopropyl)methylsiloxane] (PMMS) in various soft lithography applications. PMMS stamps were made by cross-linking with triallyl cyanurate and ethoxylated (4) bisphenol A dimethacrylate via thiol\u2212ene mixed-mode chemistry. The surface chemistry of the materials was characterized by XPS when varied from hydrophilic through oxygen plasma treatment, to hydrophobic by exposure to a fluorinated trichlorosilane agent. The materials are transparent above 300 nm and thermally stable up to 225 \u00b0C, thus rendering them capable to be employed in step-and-flash imprint lithography, nanoimprint lithography, nanotransfer printing, and proximity-field nanopatterning. The successful pattern replication from the micrometer to sub-100 nm scale was demonstrated.","author_count":10,"author_ids":["A5007699390","A5012052318","A5020001579","A5045551635","A5064340656","A5078455808","A5109069954","A5109909868","A5111683427"],"author_institution_ids":["I154570441"],"authors":[{"id":"","name":"Luis M. Campos"},{"id":"A5007699390","name":"Tu T. Truong"},{"id":"A5111683427","name":"Dong Eun Erica Shim"},{"id":"A5078455808","name":"Michael D. Dimitriou"},{"id":"A5020001579","name":"Daniel Jay-Lee Shir"},{"id":"A5109069954","name":"Ines Meinel"},{"id":"A5012052318","name":"Jeffrey A. Gerbec"},{"id":"A5109909868","name":"H. Thomas Hahn"},{"id":"A5045551635","name":"John A. Rogers"},{"id":"A5064340656","name":"Craig J. Hawker"}],"ca_any_author":true,"ca_led":true,"cited_by_count":77,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1021/cm902506a","domain_tags":["analytical_methods","plastic_associated_chemicals","wastewater_and_treatment"],"facet_tags":[],"institution_ids":["I154570441","I157725225","I161318765"],"is_oa":true,"journal":"Chemistry of Materials","last_updated":"2026-11-07T11:25:07.637180","license":"","needs_review":false,"oa_status":"green","oa_url":"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1875074","openalex_topics":["T11471","T11737","T12224"],"plastic_related":false,"plastic_tier":"chemicals-in-plastics","primary_topic":"T12224","pub_date":"2009-10-14","study_type":"article","title":"Applications of Photocurable PMMS Thiol\u2212Ene Stamps in Soft Lithography","type":"article","work_id":"W2024495322","year":2009},{"abstract":"Recently, polyesters based on the diol monomer 2,2,4,4-tetramethyl-1,3-cyclobutanediol (TMCBDO) have been shown to exhibit excellent thermal stability, mechanical properties, and optical clarity. In particular, the ability of TMCBDO to replace bisphenol A as a diol monomer in polycarbonates and polyesters has resulted in significant commercial and academic interest in these types of monomers. Herein, we report a versatile synthetic strategy based on the dimerization of ketenes derived from the thermal treatment of Meldrum's acid for the synthesis of structurally diverse cyclobutanediol (CBDO) monomers. This strategy allows a library of CBDO monomers amenable to standard polyester polymerization procedures to be prepared and the structural diversity of these CBDO monomers provides polymers with tunable physical properties, such as glass transition temperature ranging from 120 to 230 \u00b0C. The versatility and modularity of this Meldrum's acid-based approach to substituted cyclobutanediols, combined with the ease of synthesis, will be important for the further development of high-performance polyester materials that are not based on bisphenol A.","author_count":9,"author_ids":["A5003026330","A5027333730","A5030220853","A5053586495","A5062519013","A5064340656","A5067049646","A5091443355"],"author_institution_ids":["I134085113","I154570441"],"authors":[{"id":"","name":"Daniel J. Burke"},{"id":"A5091443355","name":"Takehiro Kawauchi"},{"id":"A5067049646","name":"Matthew J. Kade"},{"id":"A5062519013","name":"Frank A. Leibfarth"},{"id":"A5027333730","name":"Brenden McDearmon"},{"id":"A5053586495","name":"Martin Wolffs"},{"id":"A5030220853","name":"Paul H. Kierstead"},{"id":"A5003026330","name":"Bongjin Moon"},{"id":"A5064340656","name":"Craig J. Hawker"}],"ca_any_author":true,"ca_led":true,"cited_by_count":42,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1021/mz300497m","domain_tags":["analytical_methods","human_exposure_and_health_risk","plastic_associated_chemicals","wastewater_and_treatment"],"facet_tags":[],"institution_ids":["I134085113","I136259955","I148751991","I154570441"],"is_oa":false,"journal":"ACS Macro Letters","last_updated":"2026-11-07T11:25:07.637180","license":"","needs_review":false,"oa_status":"closed","oa_url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/mz300497m","openalex_topics":["T10405","T10661","T12084"],"plastic_related":true,"plastic_tier":"chemicals-in-plastics","primary_topic":"T10405","pub_date":"2012-10-09","study_type":"article","title":"Ketene-Based Route to rigid Cyclobutanediol Monomers for the Replacement of BPA in High Performance Polyesters","type":"article","work_id":"W2321215647","year":2012},{"abstract":"Covalent adaptable networks (CANs) typically require external catalysts for efficient cross-linker exchange, which can limit network reprocessability due to catalyst leaching and degradation. In this study, catalysts were avoided by using a bicyclo[3.3.1]nonane bis-alkyl halide cross-linker with sulfur-atom neighboring group participation (NGP) to increase the rate of bond exchange. Stress relaxation analyses demonstrate that the resultant pyridine-based network has an Arrhenius dependence on viscous flow at elevated temperatures (130\u2013170 \u00b0C), which arises from S N 1 transalkylation exchange. This thermally mediated cross-link interchange and associated flow behavior enabled reprocessing of the ionic networks over multiple damage and repair cycles. Additionally, these NGP-based CANs are chemically recyclable, allowing for recovery of the pyridyl-based polymer starting material, which comprises >90 wt % of the parent network. The dual thermal and chemical recycling potential of this catalyst-free CAN platform addresses key criteria for designing thermosets with extended lifecycles.","author_count":7,"author_ids":["A5001435522","A5006028538","A5036216864","A5044252746","A5064340656","A5069441535","A5091065613"],"author_institution_ids":["I142207940","I154570441"],"authors":[{"id":"A5006028538","name":"Lindsay L. Robinson"},{"id":"A5069441535","name":"Eden S. Taddese"},{"id":"A5044252746","name":"Jeffrey L. Self"},{"id":"A5001435522","name":"Christopher M. Bates"},{"id":"A5036216864","name":"Javier Read de Alaniz"},{"id":"A5091065613","name":"Zhishuai Geng"},{"id":"A5064340656","name":"Craig J. Hawker"}],"ca_any_author":true,"ca_led":true,"cited_by_count":21,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.macromol.2c01618","domain_tags":["analytical_methods","fate_transport_and_sorption"],"facet_tags":[],"institution_ids":["I142207940","I154570441"],"is_oa":false,"journal":"Macromolecules","last_updated":"2026-10-22T18:32:59.640997","license":"","needs_review":false,"oa_status":"closed","oa_url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.macromol.2c01618","openalex_topics":["T10405","T10810","T11145"],"plastic_related":false,"plastic_tier":"core","primary_topic":"T10810","pub_date":"2022-10-19","study_type":"article","title":"Neighboring Group Participation in Ionic Covalent Adaptable Networks","type":"article","work_id":"W4306811487","year":2022},{"abstract":"Abstract The ability to revert polymers to their original monomers represents a crucial chemical recycling technique, promoting sustainability and offering the chance to convert used materials into valuable products. In recent years, numerous studies have explored the use of polymers synthesized via reversible deactivation radical polymerization (RDRP) techniques to facilitate efficient depolymerization reactions. Herein, we report the use of a photocatalyst, zinc tetraphenylporphyrin (ZnTPP), along with light irradiation to accelerate depolymerization of polymers prepared by reversible addition\u2010fragmentation chain transfer (RAFT) polymerization. We explore various parameters affecting depolymerization efficiency, including solvents, reaction temperature (80, 100, and 120\u00b0C), the presence of photocatalysts (ZnTPP and Eosin Y), and the type of RAFT end\u2010groups, namely trithiocarbonate, dithiobenzoate, and 1H\u2010pyrazole\u20101\u2010carbodithioate. For instance, when PMMA was diluted to 25 mM in 1,4\u2010dioxane and heated to 120\u00b0C under green light irradiation in the presence of ZnTPP (200 ppm), rapid depolymerization exceeding 70% occurred within 1 h. Without ZnTPP, under similar conditions, the reaction required over 8 h to achieve a slightly lower yield. Furthermore, this method confers moderate oxygen tolerance to the system, enabling depolymerization to proceed without the need of deoxygenation, albeit at a lower rate and consequently lesser monomer recovery (31%).","author_count":7,"author_ids":["A5001395511","A5012323775","A5028830781","A5060577881","A5064340656","A5067466628","A5084579651"],"author_institution_ids":["I154570441"],"authors":[{"id":"A5028830781","name":"Gervase Ng"},{"id":"A5084579651","name":"Stuart W. Prescott"},{"id":"A5012323775","name":"Almar Postma"},{"id":"A5060577881","name":"Graeme Moad"},{"id":"A5064340656","name":"Craig J. Hawker"},{"id":"A5067466628","name":"Athina Anastasaki"},{"id":"A5001395511","name":"Cyrille Andre Boyer"}],"ca_any_author":true,"ca_led":false,"cited_by_count":33,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1002/pol.20240255","domain_tags":["analytical_methods"],"facet_tags":[],"institution_ids":["I1292875679","I154570441","I31746571","I35440088","I4210154771","I4405257276"],"is_oa":true,"journal":"Journal of Polymer Science","last_updated":"2026-11-10T11:39:43.021295","license":"cc-by","needs_review":false,"oa_status":"hybrid","oa_url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdfdirect/10.1002/pol.20240255","openalex_topics":["T10389","T10405","T10810"],"plastic_related":true,"plastic_tier":"core","primary_topic":"T10405","pub_date":"2024-05-22","study_type":"article","title":"Enhancing photothermal depolymerization with metalloporphyrin catalyst","type":"article","work_id":"W4398245957","year":2024},{"abstract":"Polymer recycling is currently predicated on the separation of a vast array of chemically different and immiscible polymer chains. As a result, only a relatively small fraction of plastic waste is ultimately recycled. Overcoming the repulsion of chemically dissimilar polymeric chains would enable the formation of strong, optically clear, and useful materials to be obtained from mixed plastics recycling waste. Here, we demonstrate the utility of relatively sparse ionic bonds (\u22481.0 and 6.5 mol % functionalization of a polymer backbone) coupled with a sulfonic acid-to-imidazole base proton transfer mechanism to facilitate the formation of optically clear materials in otherwise incompatible poly(dimethylsiloxane) and poly( n -butyl acrylate) mixtures. Adjusting the degree of ionic functionality spans a spectrum from nanometer-scale assemblies driven by electrostatic interactions to fully homogeneous blends with unique viscoelastic characteristics. This study highlights the complex physics of sparsely charged polymer blends and their potential impact on addressing society\u2019s pressing plastic waste challenges.","author_count":9,"author_ids":["A5001305555","A5001568631","A5036216864","A5057560264","A5064340656","A5065253425","A5066596999","A5118869461"],"author_institution_ids":["I2803209242"],"authors":[{"id":"A5065253425","name":"Jerrick Edmund"},{"id":"A5057560264","name":"Kseniia M. Karnaukh"},{"id":"A5066596999","name":"Shuyi Xie"},{"id":"","name":"Elizabeth A. Murphy"},{"id":"A5001568631","name":"Allison Abdilla"},{"id":"A5118869461","name":"Eiko Ino"},{"id":"A5036216864","name":"Javier Read de Alaniz"},{"id":"A5064340656","name":"Craig J. Hawker"},{"id":"A5001305555","name":"Rachel A. Segalman"}],"ca_any_author":true,"ca_led":true,"cited_by_count":6,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.macromol.5c00274","domain_tags":["analytical_methods"],"facet_tags":[],"institution_ids":["I2803209242"],"is_oa":false,"journal":"Macromolecules","last_updated":"2026-10-28T10:33:24.094632","license":"","needs_review":false,"oa_status":"closed","oa_url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.macromol.5c00274","openalex_topics":["T10389","T10405","T11471"],"plastic_related":true,"plastic_tier":"core","primary_topic":"T10389","pub_date":"2025-07-08","study_type":"article","title":"Compatibilization of Immiscible Polymer Blends through Pendant Ionic Interactions","type":"article","work_id":"W4412132509","year":2025}]}
